  "machina": {
    page: {"body":["In incremental sheet forming a tool pushes a clamped sheet into shape contour by contour, and when the clamp comes off the part springs back: shallower and bent, away from what you asked for.","A C++17 solver runs the whole cycle with frictional contact and Hill48 / Chaboche plasticity at finite strain. A surrogate trained on those runs then searches for the corrected shape without paying for a simulation.","On eight held-out parts the mean vertical RMS shape error falls from 0.300 mm uncorrected to 0.189 mm, with the edge pass, the surrogate and the optimizer together."],"choices":["The surrogate carries uncertainty and an out-of-distribution check.","An edge pass, because 0.45 mm of error sat where no tool path could reach.","One forming run per part on the test set, so the numbers are not tuned."]},
    brief: "A C++17 sheet-forming solver with a springback-compensation pipeline and an ML surrogate trained on its simulations.",
    problem: "In robotic incremental sheet forming a tool pushes a clamped sheet into shape contour by contour, and when the tool leaves and the clamp comes off the part springs back: shallower and bent, away from the shape you asked for. The usual fix is trial and error — form, scan, correct the tool path, form again. I wanted to replace those loops with computation, so that the first part comes out right.",
    built: "Three pieces that work together. A C++17 forming solver (sparlab_form) with moving rigid tools, frictional contact and Hill48 / Chaboche plasticity at finite strain, which runs the whole cycle: form, unload, release onto supports. A Python pipeline (precomp) that turns a target part into tool paths, runs the solver, and applies displacement-adjustment compensation. And a surrogate layer (precomp.ml) of MLP and gradient-boosted ensembles trained on those simulations, carrying uncertainty, an out-of-distribution check, a transfer model for a new process, and an optimiser that searches for the corrected shape on the surrogate instead of on the solver.",
    how: [
      "Forming: the sheet is a shell of solid elements; the tool is a rigid sphere swept along a contour path. Contact is enforced by penalty with Coulomb friction, plasticity is Hill48 anisotropic yield with Chaboche kinematic hardening at finite strain, and the analysis runs implicitly through form, unload and release onto a three-point support so the released shape is invariant under rigid motion.",
      "Compensation, the classical way: form the target in simulation, measure how far the released part misses, and push the tool path the other way by that much. One round costs two simulations; a fine-mesh part takes 45 to 90 minutes of CPU each, which is exactly what makes the loop expensive.",
      "The surrogate: features describing the target geometry and the process go into an ensemble that predicts the deviation field with a standard deviation, calibrated so the interval means something, and gated by a distance check so a part unlike anything in training is refused rather than guessed. An optimiser then searches the corrected shape against the surrogate, which is why the correction costs one forming run and not two.",
      "A new process without a new data set: a transfer model keeps the base surrogate and learns a linear or boosted correction on top of it, so adding the underside edge pass took eight extra simulations rather than a full retrain.",
      "Verification first: the contact tangent against finite differences to 7.2e-10, a flat punch’s force against the penalty-in-series solution to 4.5e-12, Coulomb friction along the slip direction to 7e-16, the release invariant under rigid motion to 1.1e-17 m, and the springback of an elastoplastic beam against the exact elastic unloading of its moment-curvature relation.",
    ],
    contribution: "I built this project myself, end to end: the forming solver and its contact and plasticity, the compensation pipeline, the surrogates and their calibration, the transfer model and optimiser, the benchmark campaign and the write-up.",
    challenges: [
      "The error that tool paths cannot reach. Most of what was left after correction sat at the top edge by the clamp, 0.45 mm of it, and no change to the tool path fixed it because the tool cannot get under there. Adding a second tool that sweeps the edge from the underside took that region to 0.13 mm, and the ML then corrected the rest.",
      "The optimiser exploits the model it is given. Searching the corrected shape against a surrogate means any place the surrogate is wrong is a place the optimiser will happily go: it predicted 0.14 mm and the simulations delivered 0.19. Retraining on the optimiser’s own shapes made the predictions more honest and the parts slightly worse (0.203 mm), which is recorded rather than quietly dropped.",
      "Explicit against implicit. The explicit forming path converges to the implicit springback only as the equivalent tool speed falls — 23 %, 9 %, 4 % and 1.3 % error at 28, 14, 7 and 3.5 m/s — and the whole cone at 4 m/s takes 46 minutes on two threads. That makes the fast path a feasibility and cost run, not a result, and it is labelled as one.",
      "Knowing what the numbers are not. Every figure in this project is a simulation; nothing has been compared with a formed and scanned part. The parts are small (15 to 20 mm, 2 to 4 mm deep), one material, one tool, and eight test parts is a small sample. All of it is stated at the top of the README rather than at the bottom of an appendix.",
    ],
    results: [
      "On eight held-out test parts — domes, cones, elliptic cones and pyramids in 1 mm AA5754-O — one forming run per part: 0.300 mm mean vertical RMS shape error with no correction, 0.263 mm after a full simulate-and-correct round costing two simulations, 0.248 mm from the ML first shot, and 0.189 mm with the underside edge pass, the ML and the optimiser together.",
      "37 % less error than no correction, from a single forming run — better than the two-simulation FE round, at half the simulations. It beats the plain ML first shot on seven of the eight parts.",
      "The transfer model learned the new edge-pass process from eight extra simulations rather than a retrain, taking its prediction error on held-out parts from 0.53 mm to 0.12 mm.",
      "The forming solver is verified against exact answers before any of this: contact tangent 7.2e-10, punch force 4.5e-12, friction 7e-16, release invariance 1.1e-17 m, and elastoplastic beam springback converging to 5.6e-4 with sixteen elements through the depth.",
      "375 C++ test cases and 22 Python test modules; the physics audit covers mesh convergence, the process study and validation against published deep-drawing measurements.",
    ],
    tech: [
      "C++17",
      "Eigen (SuiteSparse optional)",
      "Hill48 anisotropic yield, Chaboche kinematic hardening",
      "Penalty contact with Coulomb friction, finite strain",
      "Implicit and explicit forming paths",
      "Python: NumPy, SciPy, scikit-learn",
      "PyTorch MLP ensembles, gradient-boosted ensembles",
      "Calibrated uncertainty and an out-of-distribution gate",
      "Transfer models and surrogate-based optimisation",
      "CMake, Ninja, pytest",
    ],
  },
